From: XZ-Mods <local>
Subject: [PATCH] target/arm: make SEV/WFE wakeups MTTCG-safe and targeted

In QEMU 11.1, an ARM SEV issued by one MTTCG vCPU can race a second
vCPU's transition into the BQL-protected halt-condition wait after WFE.
The event bit is set, but the condvar broadcast can occur before the target
actually waits; the target then sleeps until an unrelated wakeup.

Serialize the event-bit update and the wakeup with the BQL, matching the
upstream lost-WFE-wakeup correction.  This is required by 32-bit Linux SMP
spinlocks, which use WFE while acquiring and DSB; SEV while releasing.

Do not host-kick vCPUs that are running.  SEV is a sticky event, so a running
vCPU consumes it at its next WFE without a thread exit.  Only an already
halted WFE vCPU needs the condition broadcast.  WFI is deliberately excluded:
SEV wakes WFE, not WFI.

--- a/target/arm/tcg/op_helper.c
+++ b/target/arm/tcg/op_helper.c
@@ -475,11 +475,36 @@
 void HELPER(sev)(CPUARMState *env)
 {
     CPUState *cs = env_cpu(env);
+
+    /*
+     * A SEV makes a halted WFE CPU runnable by changing the state tested
+     * by arm_cpu_has_work().  Serialize that change and the wakeup with the
+     * BQL: otherwise the target can test its old state, miss a condvar
+     * broadcast, and sleep after the event has been posted.
+     *
+     * This is the upstream ARM MTTCG lost-WFE-wakeup fix, backported to the
+     * 11.1 helper signature used by this tree.
+     */
+    BQL_LOCK_GUARD();
     CPU_FOREACH(cs) {
         ARMCPU *target_cpu = ARM_CPU(cs);
+
         target_cpu->env.event_register = true;
-        if (!qemu_cpu_is_self(cs)) {
+
+        /*
+         * SEV is a sticky event, not an interrupt.  A running vCPU will
+         * consume this at its next WFE without needing a host-thread exit.
+         * Only a vCPU that is already sleeping in WFE needs its halt
+         * condition broadcast.  Besides avoiding unnecessary MTTCG kicks on
+         * every guest spin_unlock(), this deliberately does not wake WFI:
+         * ARM specifies that SEV wakes WFE only.
+         *
+         * The BQL above closes the transition into qemu_cond_wait(), so this
+         * test and the conditional broadcast cannot lose an event.
+         */
+        if (!qemu_cpu_is_self(cs) &&
+            qatomic_read(&target_cpu->env.halt_reason) == HALT_WFE) {
             qemu_cpu_kick(cs);
         }
     }
 }
